               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate  calendar="#gregorian" notAfter="1399" notBefore="1200">s. xiv, and (fols. 219--228) s. xiii</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#e b"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_137">Chester, Cheshire.
                        Franciscan convent</orgName><p>Erased at foot of fol. 1r: 'De [ . . . ]'.
                        This inscription is clearly in the same form and the same hand as that in
                        Gray's Inn MS 11, so read: 'De communitate fratrum minorum cestrie. per
                        fratrem Rad' Wyche'.</p><p>The medieval binding and style of binding
                        furniture is the same on Gray's Inn, MSS 1--9, 11--12, and 23, identifiably
                        deriving from the same library. MSS 1, 2, 5, 7, 11, 12 contain ex libris
                        inscriptions.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">See Catalogue, p. 2 and cf. MS.
                     11.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Wyche's name occurs in several places: he
                     appears to have been the scribe of at least part of the MS. (see fol. 171r: '.
                     . . verba salutis beati francisci . . . script' per ff. Rad'
                     Wych'').</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">The Bostock family of Tattenhall
                     (Cheshire).</provenance>
               </history>
            </msDesc>
